Prototype Design Pattern (Java)
1. Definition

The Prototype Design Pattern is a creational design pattern that creates new objects
by copying an existing object (prototype), instead of creating them from scratch.

When to use:

Object creation is expensive.
You want to create copies of existing objects.
You want to avoid repeating complex initialization logic.

2. Basic Java Example

class Report implements Cloneable {
    String college;
    String department;

    Report(String college, String department) {
        this.college = college;
        this.department = department;
    }

    @Override
    public Report clone() {
        try {
            return (Report) super.clone();
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new AssertionError(e);
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Report original =
            new Report("Aditya University", "CSE");

        Report copy1 = original.clone();
        Report copy2 = original.clone();

        copy1.department = "AI&ML";

        System.out.println(original.department);
        System.out.println(copy1.department);
        System.out.println(copy2.department);
    }
}

Output:
CSE
AI&ML
CSE

Explanation:

  implements Cloneable:     Allows cloning through Object.clone().
  super.clone():            Creates a shallow copy of the object.
  copy1 and copy2:          Separate objects copied from original.
  Changing copy1.department does not change the other objects' fields.

3. Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy:
Shallow Copy:

  Creates a new outer object, but references to nested objects may be shared.
  Report copy = original.clone();
  
Deep Copy:

 Creates a new outer object and independently copies the nested mutable objects that need to be isolated.

Remember: super.clone() alone creates a shallow copy, not a deep copy.

4. Advantages
     Avoids recreating complex objects from scratch.
     Can reduce repeated initialization work.
     Makes it easy to create variations of existing objects.
5. Disadvantages
     Deep copying nested objects can be complicated.
     Shallow copying may accidentally share mutable state.
     Java's Cloneable and clone() mechanism has design limitations.
6. Real-World Examples
    Copying document templates.
    Duplicating preconfigured game characters.
    Creating copies of configuration objects.
    Duplicating existing graphical shapes.
